**user**:
> I am producing a bunch of figures in ggplot, but the editor wants to have the raw data for each plot as well (so they can presumably remake all the figures). Is there a way to extract this from the ggplot object?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can extract the raw data from a `ggplot` object. A `ggplot` object contains the associated data frame, and you can access it using the `ggplot_build()` function. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do it:
> 
> 1. Create your `ggplot` object as usual.
> 2. Use `ggplot_build()` to access the underlying data.
> 3. Depending on your plot, extract the data from the appropriate structure in the `ggplot` object.
> 
> Here's an example:
> 
> ```R
> library(ggplot2)
> 
> # Example ggplot
> p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) + geom_point()
> 
> # Extract the data used in the plot
> plot_data <- ggplot_build(p)$data[[1]]
> 
> # View the raw data
> print(plot_data)
> ```
> 
> In this example, `plot_data` would contain the data frame used for plotting, including any transformations applied during the ggplot processes. You can then export this data to a file format that the editor prefers, such as CSV, using `write.csv()`:
> 
> ```R
> write.csv(plot_data, "plot_raw_data.csv", row.names = FALSE)
> ```
> 
> This will give your editor the underlying data needed to recreate the figures.
